  "image_name": "9a2e83570a8ae9273ac36a2121fa8a29.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three electrochemical reactor designs for converting CO₂: a microfluidic electrolyser, a zero-gap membrane electrolyser, and a hybrid electrolyser. All three show CO₂ entering on the cathode side near a catalyst and gas-diffusion electrode, while oxygen leaves from the anode side and reduction products exit from the cathode side.\n\nThe main contrast is how liquid electrolyte and membrane separation are arranged. In the microfluidic design, a relatively large liquid electrolyte chamber separates the anode from the cathode region, and a reference electrode is inserted into the liquid. This layout appears simple but leaves a long ionic path through bulk electrolyte. In the zero-gap membrane design, the electrodes are pressed closely around a membrane, minimizing the gap and separating anolyte and cathode products more directly. The hybrid design combines features of both: it keeps flowing liquid compartments, labeled anolyte and catholyte, but also includes a membrane between them, while still feeding CO₂ to a gas-diffusion cathode.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes different cell architectures and fluid pathways, highlighting tradeoffs between bulk-liquid operation, membrane-separated zero-gap geometry, and an intermediate hybrid configuration.",
